{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound","id":718557,"title":"Apeal of the unbound?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-23T04:06:50-07:00","id":718557,"title":"Apeal of the unbound?","content":"on a game I run on rpol get inudated with unbound apps, and want to ask WHAT IS THE APEAL<br /> <br /> mechanicaly they have NO advantages<br /> <br /> and does not seem NEARLY as cool themanticly as the invictus or Circle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T06:20:57-07:00","id":718750,"title":"","content":"Unbound do actually have a mechanical advantage. They aren't under any obligation to kowtow to covenant or city officials based on affiliations. Mechanically this is reflected in the fact that other PCs and NPCs cannot use status against them. (Which often leads to stilted roleplaying where they are expected to know someone's standing despite not actually playing in the setting before.) If you're using 2nd edition rules, this renders them immune to the Carthian Law powers which entail stripping away a victim's status from them or allowing the Carthian to bludgeon their opponents with the Carthian's own status, and saps the effectiveness of disciplines that augment social rolls (notably Majesty and Nightmare).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T06:46:26-07:00","id":718827,"title":"","content":"In your case I'd guess it's probably a way to avoid starting at the low end of a hierarchy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T06:47:21-07:00","id":718828,"title":"","content":"Covenants are basically glorified cults of monsters. Joining one is a Humanity breaking point. The stuff your covenant superiors encourage you to do (and, by encourage, I mean order) tends to hurt your Humanity more. So, avoiding all those breaking poitns is one advantage. <br /> <br /> A second advantage is freedom. Even the anarchic Circle and the most liberal Carthians still have the desire to control those around them. Unbound aren't tied to serve any of the covenant elders.<br /> <br /> Both are major advantages.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T08:44:18-07:00","id":719402,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tsusasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=718750#post718750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">. (Which often leads to stilted roleplaying where they are expected to know someone's standing despite not actually playing in the setting before.)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This right here is the reason I usually play an unbound character. I really dislike it when we are in the middle of something and the story teller is like &quot;oh by the way you are suppose to know and respect so and so because he is high ranking in such and such and you'd know all this cool stuff&quot; but.... i dont. So I usually avoid that and avoid being told how my character is suppose to feel about another character/npc.<br /> <br /> Secondly, Even though the covenants are really cool and have a lot of neat mechanical things they can do, I have never built a vampire PC ( lots of NPCs ) whose personality fit with a covenant. Breaking points are a huge negative.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2308,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","user":"Vitalis","date":". 10-23-2015, 12:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T09:08:14-07:00","id":719512,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tsusasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=718750#post718750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unbound do actually have a mechanical advantage. They aren't under any obligation to kowtow to covenant or city officials based on affiliations. Mechanically this is reflected in the fact that other PCs and NPCs cannot use status against them. (Which often leads to stilted roleplaying where they are expected to know someone's standing despite not actually playing in the setting before.) If you're using 2nd edition rules, this renders them immune to the Carthian Law powers which entail stripping away a victim's status from them or allowing the Carthian to bludgeon their opponents with the Carthian's own status, and saps the effectiveness of disciplines that augment social rolls (notably Majesty and Nightmare).</div> </div> </div> </div>So carthian powers only affect other carthians?<br /> I was under impression that a Carthian prefect(prince ) could use Carthian Law on any kindred?<br /> In fact I thought one power affected city status.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you want By Night Studios to release new LARP RULES for NWoD,<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/conventions-and-gatherings/313137-open-letter-to-by-night-studios\" target=\"_blank\">like this post</a>.<br /> <br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=62\" data-tempid=\"temp_62_1418088885567_853\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/594-chattnos","name":"Chattnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"879"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T09:27:06-07:00","id":719612,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chattnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=719512#post719512\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So carthian powers only affect other carthians?<br /> I was under impression that a Carthian prefect(prince ) could use Carthian Law on any kindred?<br /> In fact I thought one power affected city status.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're correct. A hypothetical Carthian Law that stripped an offender of their Covenant status wouldn't work on an unaligned, but I don't recall a Merit for such a law actually existing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T09:54:03-07:00","id":719713,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tsusasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=718750#post718750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unbound do actually have a mechanical advantage. They aren't under any obligation to kowtow to covenant or city officials based on affiliations. Mechanically this is reflected in the fact that other PCs and NPCs cannot use status against them. (Which often leads to stilted roleplaying where they are expected to know someone's standing despite not actually playing in the setting before.) If you're using 2nd edition rules, this renders them immune to the Carthian Law powers which entail stripping away a victim's status from them or allowing the Carthian to bludgeon their opponents with the Carthian's own status, and saps the effectiveness of disciplines that augment social rolls (notably Majesty and Nightmare).</div> </div> </div> </div>Very broadly speaking, do you think that's balanced against being denied blood magics, especially in early play? <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I always felt that some kind of merit reflecting their ability to navigate unimpeded through many political tangles and play the Big Five off each other would have been welcome, at least, especially for people who played non-political characters because they're not good at politics.</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T10:48:09-07:00","id":719852,"title":"","content":"Of course, the flip-side to &quot;freedom&quot; is lack of protection, and you do still have to respect Domain officials, like the Prince, unless you want Final Death. I personally think those outweigh the advantages, but the &quot;freedom&quot; bit is probably the most common rationale I've heard.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T10:54:21-07:00","id":719896,"title":"","content":"I'd guess it's for the same reason that the freeform games based in the old White Wolf chat (and a number of spin-off places) were always overrun with Anarchs. A bunch of people want to play badass loners beholden to no one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T11:15:41-07:00","id":719932,"title":"","content":"A mechanical incentive to group up a little tighter, perhaps?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T15:16:54-07:00","id":720723,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=719713#post719713\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Very broadly speaking, do you think that's balanced against being denied blood magics, especially in early play? <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I always felt that some kind of merit reflecting their ability to navigate unimpeded through many political tangles and play the Big Five off each other would have been welcome, at least, especially for people who played non-political characters because they're not good at politics.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>Blood magic and other such covenant advantages are pretty self-balancing, because of the way experience works. Sure, an Unaligned character doesn't have access to the same advantages as their Circle of the Crone buddy. But every experience the Acolyte character spends on Cruac is an experience they're not spending on something else. So ultimately by going Unaligned, you're trading options and security for specialization and liberty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T15:58:29-07:00","id":720865,"title":"","content":"Being unaligned is pretty unattractive unless you really can't stomach the covenants. You have not built in friends or enfranchisement. you're not tied down either, to place or people. <br /> <br /> Honestly, I think Unbound and Apostates in Mage 2e map pretty nicely to each other. Sure you're beholden to nobody, but you lack resources, friends and protection. That's a pretty big disadvantage when you're competing for the same food sources as other Kindred.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T11:48:22-07:00","id":723826,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=720723#post720723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Blood magic and other such covenant advantages are pretty self-balancing, because of the way experience works. Sure, an Unaligned character doesn't have access to the same advantages as their Circle of the Crone buddy. But every experience the Acolyte character spends on Cruac is an experience they're not spending on something else. So ultimately by going Unaligned, you're trading options and security for specialization and liberty.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I generally agree with the &quot;Power=Power; the kind doesn't matter as much as you might think&quot; sentiment, blood magic is just <i>so </i>versatile and iconic. I freely agree that it's not illogical, or even very unbalanced, but having that denied, specifically, stings.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=720865#post720865\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure you're beholden to nobody, but you lack resources, friends and protection. That's a pretty big disadvantage when you're competing for the same food sources as other Kindred.</div> </div> </div> </div>While the Covenant dots are helpful, I wouldn't consider their absence crippling to survival- Herd is cheap, unless you're Gilded Cage-ing feeding territories.<br /> <br /> -sigh-<br /> <br /> I think I have it. Unbound isn't a particular mechanical disadvantage, but only because the vampires are PCs. For an NPC, those resources would be the foundation of their kindred career, the only support they'd ever be able to truly count on. A player at a table can expect to be invited to aid in adventures, even if it doesn't come directly to them from high, because gamers have a gentleman's understanding recognizing that time allocation and infighting are harsh mistresses. A Player playing an Unbounded very often still gets experience, play time, and help with their own projects too (I have yet to play a table where anybody played their character as a Darwinian monster with their <i>own </i>group). A Coterie is the Unbounded's mini-Covenant. I suppose no free Herd or Allies or magic is a small price to pay when you're still getting conditional access to them through your buds.<br /> <br /> ...I <i>do </i>still feel that an Unbounded should get something for dealing with Covenants (specifically for when they don't <i>want </i>to deal with Covenants), if just for a tiny acknowledgement that nonpolitical isn't entirely nonpolitical when your whole community eats and breathes and sleeps intrigue. Plus, it would be nice to have some degree of acknowledgement that, lacking common ground with the broad, sweeping motivations of the Covenants doesn't mean you Did It Wrong not to have gone with one (you want blood magic? Political office? Duh, should've paid lip service to a Covenant, it's meant to be the default anyway), it just means that whatever your priorities are, they're at least as important as those resources or grand philosophies- not because they're <i>big</i>, but because they're <i>yours</i>.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1289,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","user":"Hastur00","date":". 10-24-2015, 02:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T12:21:26-07:00","id":723923,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=723826#post723826\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I generally agree with the &quot;Power=Power; the kind doesn't matter as much as you might think&quot; sentiment, blood magic is just <i>so </i>versatile and iconic. I freely agree that it's not illogical, or even very unbalanced, but having that denied, specifically, stings.<br /> <br /> <br /> While the Covenant dots are helpful, I wouldn't consider their absence crippling to survival- Herd is cheap, unless you're Gilded Cage-ing feeding territories.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're conflating the mechanical experience value with narrative, which isn't ideal. In a city where you're Unbound and you have no friends, you can theoretically purchase that Feeding Grounds merit anywhere, right? But because you're not respected thanks to your lack of association, people feel more comfortable being aggressive about your territory. They're not worried about the reprisal you can bring to bear because you have no friends. So your access to the merit is fraught with tension.<br /> <br /> That can be a lot of fun, but it should be understood that in character, that's a pretty big disadvantage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=723826#post723826\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-sigh-<br /> <br /> I think I have it. Unbound isn't a particular mechanical disadvantage, but only because the vampires are PCs. For an NPC, those resources would be the foundation of their kindred career, the only support they'd ever be able to truly count on. A player at a table can expect to be invited to aid in adventures, even if it doesn't come directly to them from high, because gamers have a gentleman's understanding recognizing that time allocation and infighting are harsh mistresses. A Player playing an Unbounded very often still gets experience, play time, and help with their own projects too (I have yet to play a table where anybody played their character as a Darwinian monster with their <i>own </i>group). A Coterie is the Unbounded's mini-Covenant. I suppose no free Herd or Allies or magic is a small price to pay when you're still getting conditional access to them through your buds.</div> </div> </div> </div>For NPCs, having a coterie alleviates some of the burdens of being Unbound, too. The key is that it's going to be harder to find people willing to roll with you when they have absolutely no reason to get together with you. If I'm an Invictus Kindred, there's a reason for me to form a coterie with other Invictus Kindred, because our pooled resources will make us more powerful in the Covenant, and because we THEORETICALLY won't screw each other over because we're from the same group. That same Invictus Kindred also has reasons to align with the Carthian Kindred and the Crone Kindred, because their resources aren't available to my fellow Invictus. There's tension there because it's not totally clear if we can trust each other, but also valuable things and some sense that we've all bought into this idea of Kindred society and stuff like that. The Unbound don't even do that, and they don't necessarily have anything unique to offer, so it makes sense that they're treated as these nomadic, vagrant types that the Kindred of the city can't trust. You can't trust someone that's unmoored, because they're not at all invested in the systems that keep the Kindred from killing each other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=723826#post723826\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...I <i>do </i>still feel that an Unbounded should get something for dealing with Covenants (specifically for when they don't <i>want </i>to deal with Covenants), if just for a tiny acknowledgement that nonpolitical isn't entirely nonpolitical when your whole community eats and breathes and sleeps intrigue.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure I follow. You're Unbound, so you're gonna get treated like an unbound person. This will undoubtedly lead to Conditions, beats and drama. All good stuff. If you're selecting to play one of the Unbound, you're obviously interested in exploring what it's like to not have affiliation in a society which is highly factionalized. It's interesting and fun.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=723826#post723826\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Plus, it would be nice to have some degree of acknowledgement that, lacking common ground with the broad, sweeping motivations of the Covenants doesn't mean you Did It Wrong not to have gone with one (you want blood magic? Political office? Duh, should've paid lip service to a Covenant, it's meant to be the default anyway), it just means that whatever your priorities are, they're at least as important as those resources or grand philosophies- not because they're <i>big</i>, but because they're <i>yours</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>The game makes no attempt to suggest you're doing it wrong by not joining a Covenant. In fact, Requiem doesn't take a moral stance on the objective necessity of Covenants at all. It doesn't justify them morally. They exist, whether that's good or bad is up to you.<br /> <br /> One could actually argue that the game implies that Covenants are a bit unhealthy for you Humanity-wise, since joining one is actually a Breaking Point against Humanity 5. I don't think it's suggesting that they're bad by doing that though, just making it clear that joining an organization made for Kindred, by Kindred, with Kindred aims which wholly do not align with human goals, you're running the risk of losing Humanity.<br /> <br /> The reasons people join the Covenants are numerous, but undoubtedly the most common of ALL is that you just don't want to be alone in the All-Night Society. Not every member of the Invictus is a suit-wearing libertarian shark, just as not every member of the Sanctified is going to be a book burning zealot. If you specifically chose to avoid associating with any of the Covenants in a given domain, that is actually a <i>very </i>political statement, and it's no wonder that vampires in those Covenants might seek to disenfranchise you.<br /> <br /> But really, it comes back to the drama of being Unbound. The game is not telling you it's a bad thing. It's a dramatic thing, arguably a very interesting thing, provided people are interested in exploring the reasons and consequences for not associating with the Covenants.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T13:12:34-07:00","id":724055,"title":"","content":"Prince of the Night, is this for a regular game or a larger role-playing thing?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T13:33:19-07:00","id":724071,"title":"","content":"First of all, I agree completely with the first two points- you correctly extrapolated what my summation was going for.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=723923#post723923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure I follow. You're Unbound, so you're gonna get treated like an unbound person. This will undoubtedly lead to Conditions, beats and drama. All good stuff. If you're selecting to play one of the Unbound, you're obviously interested in exploring what it's like to not have affiliation in a society which is highly factionalized. It's interesting and fun.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=723923#post723923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But really, it comes back to the drama of being Unbound. The game is not telling you it's a bad thing. It's a dramatic thing, arguably a very interesting thing, provided people are interested in exploring the reasons and consequences for not associating with the Covenants.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree, but while not necessarily unbalanced, the kinds of stories that get told about an outsider successfully weaving through or ignoring or conquering the system tend to be... uphill struggles, lone unaided effort, increment by increment of progress. All legit, absolutely, but that's a harder vibe to play 1) in a group game, for both time and the &quot;going it alone&quot; vibe, and 2) when you're foregoing the advantages of Covenant. I'm not saying it should be easy, by any lengths, but that's already a more difficult story, that's more difficult to tell, for a character that's one step away from &quot;country-club-crashing hobo who we're legally not allowed to exclude&quot;.<br /> <br /> If an Unbound is to succeed in those stories, it strikes me as peculiar that there's no means of demonstrating, mechanically, how they can take that independence and make it the strength by which they can navigate the system. Something that demonstrates that their ability to avoid assimilation isn't just a cross to bear to prove that they <i>can </i>beat the system by blood and sweat and near-compulsive monomania, but that <i>it's the reason</i> they were able to succeed at all.<br /> <br /> Just spitballing: being Unbound is about either purposefully <i>not </i>belonging, or surviving <i>despite </i>not belonging. I just think that they should have something that, while not completely (or even mostly) offsetting their difficulties, makes it clear that Covenants don't have it all their own way- an extra door to reflect that you've gotten wise to the most common recruitment/blackmail techniques, or a bonus <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">out a sheer outraged individualism bonus </span></span></span>against intimidation when being coerced to act in a Covenant's interests, or the ability to get things done more quickly because you don't have to worry about hierarchy and stamps of approval. Some quick, insouciant little wink to the fact that even when it's clear that resources and numbers will win the day, that flexibility and unpredictability were able to win you some key battles.<br /> <br /> As of now, the cost-benefit analysis of Unbound is basically &quot;lack of responsibility vs. lack of support&quot;, isn't it? How would you respond to making lack of responsibility less passive and more active? Focus less on &quot;Don't bother asking, he won't do it, we have no leverage over him&quot; and more &quot;infrequent Get Out of Jail Free card&quot;?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1289,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","user":"Hastur00","date":". 10-24-2015, 04:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T13:45:59-07:00","id":724091,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=724071#post724071\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If an Unbound is to succeed in those stories, it strikes me as peculiar that there's no means of demonstrating, mechanically, how they can take that independence and make it the strength by which they can navigate the system. Something that demonstrates that their ability to avoid assimilation isn't just a cross to bear to prove that they <i>can </i>beat the system by blood and sweat and near-compulsive monomania, but that <i>it's the reason</i> they were able to succeed at all.<br /> <br /> Just spitballing: being Unbound is about either purposefully <i>not </i>belonging, or surviving <i>despite </i>not belonging. I just think that they should have something that, while not completely (or even mostly) offsetting their difficulties, makes it clear that Covenants don't have it all their own way- an extra door to reflect that you've gotten wise to the most common recruitment/blackmail techniques, or a bonus <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">out a sheer outraged individualism bonus </span></span></span>against intimidation when being coerced to act in a Covenant's interests, or the ability to get things done more quickly because you don't have to worry about hierarchy and stamps of approval. Some quick, insouciant little wink to the fact that even when it's clear that resources and numbers will win the day, that flexibility and unpredictability were able to win you some key battles.<br /> <br /> As of now, the cost-benefit analysis of Unbound is basically &quot;lack of responsibility vs. lack of support&quot;, isn't it? How would you respond to making lack of responsibility less passive and more active? Less &quot;Don't bother asking, he won't do it, we have no leverage over him&quot; and more &quot;get out of jail free card&quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>At the risk of sounding dismissive, you're discussing the struggle of the Unbound in a way that sounds more like the Anarchs against the Camarilla than what they are in Requiem. The lack of an identity is not really an identity, there's nothing special about them to draw from. It's like being an Apostate. There's different flavors of Apostate, but being an Apostate does not confer identity or benefit all by itself.<br /> <br /> Being Unbound is not an actual identity all by itself, so there's nothing to really hang any kind of advantage on it. There isn't an advantage to not doing something, and when you get into the territory of doing something (i.e. being more than &quot;not in a Covenant&quot;) THEN there MIGHT be some kind of benefit. That's when you get into the territory of a local Covenant, though.<br /> <br /> But really, I think it should be said that you're too narrowly defining the Unbound as those who reject the system, rather than simply being those who are not a part of it. One implies a unified action, while the other is just a sort of categorization of a number of different stances. I think I made this mistake in my last post as well. The Anarchs from Masquerade are certainly the former, and fit what you're describing rather well. The Unbound are not that, though they can also encompass that.<br /> <br /> You can't systematize the Unbound identity without it ceasing to be that identity, which is why it can only by characterized by what it isn't, i.e. not belonging to a Covenant, not being beholden to a Covenant, not being seen as a participating member of the domain, etc.<br /> <br /> Really the point I'm trying to make is that once you start trying to give a Covenant Advantage to the Unbound, they cease being the Unbound and start being a local covenant. Maybe in your case that local covenant is basically the Anarchs.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T14:50:11-07:00","id":724230,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=724091#post724091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At the risk of sounding dismissive, you're discussing the struggle of the Unbound in a way that sounds more like the Anarchs against the Camarilla than what they are in Requiem. The lack of an identity is not really an identity, there's nothing special about them to draw from. It's like being an Apostate. There's different flavors of Apostate, but being an Apostate does not confer identity or benefit all by itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't even think of them, although I see exactly where you got it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I'm not exactly <i>pushing </i>for the more aggressive Anarch-style &quot;we're here, we do stuff, deal with it&quot; feel- I just think after a while, if you expect to get any politics or favor-brokering done, at <i>all</i>, even kindred who just want to spend their requiem watching TV, but who feel <i>very strongly</i> about that, will learn how to avoid stepping on unnecessary toes.<br /> <br /> But I think you're right about lack of Unbound identity. That might be where it starts- there are any number of reasons for characters to be unbound, but it's more or less assumed that they're either avoiding vampiric power structures or raging against the machine. Maybe it's the fact that between the fluff support of &quot;other Covenants recruit or ignore or use you as appropriate&quot; and a lack of Unbound-common mechanical benefits makes naturally poor soil for that purpose. I've been trying to think of more characters for whom being Unbound is an ideological or practical choice, not an apathetic one- socially and politically active, without necessarily being socially or politically active <i>with each other</i>.<br /> <br /> ...and the more I think about active vs. passive the more I see it as a spectrum, and the more I think that wouldn't make a terrible metric by which to judge access to those mechanical benefits. <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The other Covenants fill the rest of kindred life so snugly, any attempt to navigate it without them </span></span></span>while still engaging in political and social activity would by necessity have to be pretty- <i>specialized</i>.<br /> <br /> Wow. You're right. I <i>do </i>think the Unbound should be treated as its own Covenant, and not just an absence thereof.<br /> <br /> I did not know that about myself. <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Since a sixth Covenant an entirely different why-or-why-not argument, one in which I'm not nearly as invested in right now, I won't continue in that vein.</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1289,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","user":"Hastur00","date":". 10-24-2015, 08:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T15:40:09-07:00","id":724294,"title":"","content":"I mean... Isn't the &quot;I actively stand apart from Kindred politics&quot; Covenant just the Carthians?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 10-24-2015, 06:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T16:06:48-07:00","id":724322,"title":"","content":"The Carthians are weird. The LS and Invictus are very static, they embrace a very specific concept, even if it has variations. And even the Circle of the Crone is easily grasped as 'pagan' religion. The Carthians are meant to be looking for a new form of politics for the Kindred, the problem being that that can be anything from complete anarchy to communism, meritocracy to democracy. They don't follow the 'standard' neofeudal setup the Invictus does, but wherever they are, providing they're not espousing apolitics, they have some political belief. The Premier from Damnation City is a good example of a Carthian ruler with an organized Carthian city, complete with some people getting screwed. To make things weirder, they 'seem' to newcomers from Masquerade like the Anarchs and are occasionally depicted 'looking' like they espouse a certain ideal or another, like on the cover of their covenant book.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"http://vignette2.wikia.nocookie.net/whitewolf/images/a/a7/Vtrcarthians.png/revision/latest?cb=20140515221643\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br /> I asked Prince what setup his game is because when it comes to the long-running 'living' games like Wanton Wicked, I hear a lot of complaints about how there is a sort of ingrained clique and hierarchy with the older players, putting newcoming players in an unenviable position where they have to be subservient to them, providing they're in a covenant. This is just what I hear, not having played them, though I've certainly noticed similar structures of elitism in other places online.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-24-2015, 07:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T16:43:02-07:00","id":724453,"title":"","content":"Anarchy is a pretty solid political belief, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T17:25:27-07:00","id":724565,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=724230#post724230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wow. You're right. I <i>do </i>think the Unbound should be treated as its own Covenant, and not just an absence thereof.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know you're not interested in exploring it further, but I just feel it should be stressed here: If they're their own Covenant, they're no longer Unbound, and if they're a covenant, they're no longer abstaining from Kindred politics, they're engaging in it in a separatist fashion.<br /> <br /> You can't be apolitical and anti-political at the same time. Being anti-political is a political stance. Such a group also fails to capture the full variety of Unbound circumstance, which is more complex than disbelief in Kindred power structures (incidentally, a similar complaint can be lodged against people's flanderized ideas of the Covenants, such as the Sanctified or the Invictus.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T18:02:43-07:00","id":724634,"title":"","content":"Although I think in a political environment the choice to be apolitical is itself a political choice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T19:18:11-07:00","id":724751,"title":"","content":"Yeah. Really the point in trying to make is that you're not REALLY Unbound if you form a large group off Ike-minded people with the goal to abstain from Kindred society, then you're in a Covenant, it's just a newly created one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-25T10:10:17-07:00","id":726399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=724565#post724565\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I know you're not interested in exploring it further, but I just feel it should be stressed here: If they're their own Covenant, they're no longer Unbound, and if they're a covenant, they're no longer abstaining from Kindred politics, they're engaging in it in a separatist fashion.</div> </div> </div> </div>I <i>know</i>. Any attempt to build a non-Covenant that operates to function between and despite the other Covenants is, by necessity, way too closely integrated with them for an &quot;undecided&quot; box. That is unambiguously clear to me, yes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-25T11:35:39-07:00","id":726585,"title":"","content":"The advantage the Unbound get has been stated before - they can spend their experience elsewhere. For every dot that another vampire has in Carthian Law, an unbound can put in mental and social Merits that reflect their resistance to coercion and intimidation. Just because it's not spelled out as a direct benefit of being unbound doesn't mean you can't make a character with that ethos and the points to back it up.<br /> <br /> Why should an Unbound have any inherent bonus or specialization at resisting social controls than a skilled player of the political game? It's all about where you put your points, and I strongly oppose lessening the burden of being Unbound by giving them direct mechanical advantages - it cheapens the experience.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3081-anachronistic-jam","name":"Anachronistic Jam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3081_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"211"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-25T12:10:39-07:00","id":726701,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anachronistic Jam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=726585#post726585\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just because it's not spelled out as a direct benefit of being unbound doesn't mean you can't make a character with that ethos and the points to back it up.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's fair. I would get behind that. Maybe if there were more fluff to back ^that up. Some vague, indirect do-it-yourself-itude that offsets the <i>total </i>blandness of Unbound, that acknowledges yes, you're on your own, and here's why that <i>could </i>be a good thing. As written, it's just a big blank spot where you put characters who don't care or don't fit. <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"> If you're going to get pummeled by that lack of personal support or infrastructure on a nightly basis, you should at least get a nod that sometimes there is a reason that characters are here purposefully, </span></span></span>that there <i>are </i>lots of reasons for kindred to go that path.<br /> <br /> Could it be that very tabula-rasa-ness? Being Unbound that makes it a kind of dead zone for character concepts that aren't directly anarchic, apolitical, or outcast- the overwhelming nuance and mechanical benefit and story proximity of the Covenants drowns them out, which is fine, because game decisions aside, it <i>is </i>a much more individual-niche kind of political designation. <br /> <br /> Would explaining away this whole windmill-tilt as a desire for a fuller range of Unbound membership and psychology make sense in <i>that </i>context?<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-25T19:57:53-07:00","id":727927,"title":"","content":"Is it possible that joining covenents may be just a thing older vamps do?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you want By Night Studios to release new LARP RULES for NWoD,<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/conventions-and-gatherings/313137-open-letter-to-by-night-studios\" target=\"_blank\">like this post</a>.<br /> <br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=62\" data-tempid=\"temp_62_1418088885567_853\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/594-chattnos","name":"Chattnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"879"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T00:44:39-07:00","id":728077,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chattnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=727927#post727927\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is it possible that joining covenents may be just a thing older vamps do?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd actually expect it to be the other way around. Younger vampires need a family, a place to fit in, as well as the strength and protection of a possey at their back to look out for them and show them the ropes. Even if your covenant isn't a perfect fit for your mentality, it sure is nice to have &quot;people&quot; you mostly agree with to talk to and to guide you through the long nights.<br /> <br /> As you get older you get less from the covenants. You are powerful enough, skilled enough, and smart enough to survive on your own - your networks extend through both the daytime and the night, and your allies have been with you for decades or centuries. In the meantime the demands of the Covenant have increased, neonates and acolytes jockeying for time and attention keep you from your pursuits... and really, even if you still agree with your Covenant, do you really *need* it anymore? And if you have stayed Unbound for this long, why would you want to throw away your hard-fought and won neutrality, even the respect that comes with that, to be the lowest rung in a social machine that has little to offer you?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3081-anachronistic-jam","name":"Anachronistic Jam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3081_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"211"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T01:51:56-07:00","id":728166,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty sure you could easily play a mostly apolitical Kindred in the Carthians as long as you're willing to help out your Covenant-mates. The Cov book says something like &quot;The best part of the Carthians is that no one cares what you do as long as you pay your dues; the worst part is that nobody gives a shit about what you do as long as you pay your dues.&quot;<br /> <br /> Nothing to do about the appeal of the Unbound (which has been covered nicely), just a brief digression into the variety of outlooks you can find in the Carthians.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage the Awakening 2e Communal Homebrew Threads:<br /> - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/966394-perils-of-the-path-post-your-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">Perils of the Path</a> - Other homebrew threads linked in the OP!<br /> <br /> Join us at the unofficial IRC channels on.irc.sorcery.net at #exalted and now #theonyxpath!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/451-kirby-jerusalem","name":"Kirby Jerusalem","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar451_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-29T13:59:01-07:00","id":739560,"title":"","content":"In a local LARP game, there was always kind of a joke that the Unaligned/Unbound really were the sixth Covenant. Players even complained that they didn't get a &quot;good&quot; Covenant Advantage for them. There was eventually a narrative split - the Unbound were people united by the cause of rejecting being in any Covenants, who occasionally worked together to stop Covenant supremacy. Then there were the Unaligned - people who just hadn't picked a Covenant yet, or were outside of it for random reasons but had no real dog in the fight.<br /> <br /> The number one reason for people playing Unbound in that game was being afraid of Covenant connections. Either they were unsure of themselves in a structured group setting, didn't want to learn the flavor needed, they had bad experiences with previous Covenant play, or they wanted the ability to ignore anyone at anytime without mechanical consequences coming their way (not that there wouldn't be PvP or personal consequences). <br /> <br /> Starting up a Damnation City game soon with one remote player taking the role of Elder, and the local crew taking the role of his minions for street-level play. I decided to go for an Unaligned character so I could concentrate on being the Elder's minion first and foremost at game start. However, I do plan to try to &quot;be impressive&quot; so that maybe the character is recruited by a covenant, so I'm not going full Unaligned, as they might say.<br /> <br /> Major Unaligned NPCs in my longer chronicle include a Daeva who joined a custom Bloodline whose philosophy pretty much requires its members be Unbound. A group of Strix-hunting Khaibit and their Ventrue fixer who ally with whichever local Covenant makes their job easier, and a hidden embrace that the sire kept off the books/census of the Domain as an ace in the hole.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4047-holy","name":"Holy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4047_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"274"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-29T14:24:30-07:00","id":739638,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Holy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/718557-apeal-of-the-unbound?p=739560#post739560\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In a local LARP game, there was always kind of a joke that the Unaligned/Unbound really were the sixth Covenant. Players even complained that they didn't get a &quot;good&quot; Covenant Advantage for them. There was eventually a narrative split - the Unbound were people united by the cause of rejecting being in any Covenants, who occasionally worked together to stop Covenant supremacy. Then there were the Unaligned - people who just hadn't picked a Covenant yet, or were outside of it for random reasons but had no real dog in the fight.<br /> <br /> The number one reason for people playing Unbound in that game was being afraid of Covenant connections. Either they were unsure of themselves in a structured group setting, didn't want to learn the flavor needed, they had bad experiences with previous Covenant play, or they wanted the ability to ignore anyone at anytime without mechanical consequences coming their way (not that there wouldn't be PvP or personal consequences).</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it's nice to see that I'm not the only person for whom there was some... ambiguity, there, at least.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-31T23:51:26-07:00","id":745960,"title":"","content":"Think of it this way, how many people in day-to-day life are <b>not</b> actively involved with political parties, though it can certainly offer advantages to those involved and some people even have leanings towards some of their beliefs and policies?<br /> <br /> Me and my group see being Unbound as sort of the same - many people simply do not have strong enough loyalties or are interested in the degree of personal investment required to get something out of being part of the covenant. Security in numbers can be quite relative when factional rivalries come into play and sometimes being mostly invisible and without enemies can be safe enough.<br /> <br /> What doesn't stop one from being political - leanings toward one or other covenant might exist and those people, specially when quite sociable on their own, can become convenient &quot;neutral parties&quot; or intermediaries on the morass of a city's night court, among other things.<br /> <br /> Not to mention very little is truly stactic with the kindred - an Unbound vampire might actually come to identify more with a covenant or other as it ages and starts losing some of the sensibilities and interests of its mortal years, a mid-level or leading figure in one of the covenants &quot;loses the spark&quot; and goes Unbound itself as it sees old rivals getting to the top or one's view of &quot;the cause&quot; becoming more isolated and fringe within the covenant due to changing times and individuals... or either of these two kindred (if not both together), though a combination of charisma, meeting curious or dissatisfied kindred of many factions and luck ends up with a network of associates that mutates into a bloodline or new local &quot;fringe&quot; faction or &quot;heretic&quot; cult. Raphael Pope from Ressurectionists and Wicked Dead: Immortal Sinners could be seen as an example of this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 11-01-2015, 01:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}